From 32406fd7699ff20deed9b8068954d92e77d5bfa7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 10:13:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] tui_gateway: legacy hand-back and gateway claim read
 the ledger by one lineage rule; pin the restore gap

Follow-up to PR #1720 review 5888998970 (card t_01a1d44ea47c465a), three non-blocking nits:

1. One ordering rule. The legacy owner probe (_ledger_and_leases) read the ledger key-first, the gateway's
   _gateway_holds_work in sorted order. With the tip `queued` and an older root `unknown`, the legacy leg read
   `queued` and handed the chat back while the gateway read `unknown` and never claimed. Both now call
   kanban_leg_custody.lineage_ledger_status: order-independent, any non-terminal row outside
   FORWARDABLE_LEDGER_STATES answers with its status (owns nothing: the legacy leg keeps the chat), else the
   first forwardable status in sorted id order. RED on 7835e1ef98: _kb_gateway_owns answered True
   ("ledger queued") for the tip-queued/root-unknown chat the gateway does not claim.
2. The restore gap is named on gateway_owner: the legacy side cannot see the gateway's in-memory live LOCAL
   session/route, so an unrestored chat waits for its ledger work to end. A test pins it as bounded: the legacy
   leg takes the chat back within one OWNER_TTL_S and claims the notice exactly once.
3. _gateway_launch_profile is tested against a real gateway_state.json under a temp Hermes root:
   served_profiles[0], pid mismatch, missing and empty served_profiles.
